The second PUase enzyme was recovered with 0.1 M NaCl, while a third PUase proteineluted the column with 0.3 M NaCl. No further PUase activity was observed with the application ofhigher NaCl concentrations. The protein which was recovered with 0.1 M NaCl was chosen forfurther study. The 0.1 M NaCl fraction was concentrated by membrane filtration on a 10 kDaMWCO membrane and resulted in 5.0% recovery of enzymatic activity in the crude extract. Theextracellular soluble protein was purified to apparent electrophoretic homogeneity as indicated by theappearance of a single band on ND–PAGE gel. The appearance of a single zone of clearing on thePU zymogram which corresponded to the single band on the ND–PAGE gel indicates that this proteinis involved in the degradation of PU (Fig. 3
                     ). Plots of log MW versus R
                     f for the SDS–PAGE gel indicate a single 31 kDamonomeric protein (Fig. 4
                     ). The appearance of a single zone of clearing on the PU zymogram which corresponded to the bandon the SDS–PAGE gel further supports the idea of a monomeric protein of 31 kDa being involvedin the degradation of PUs.
